---
import XPWindow from './ui/XPWindow.astro';
import BadtzMaru from './ui/BadtzMaru.astro';
---

<section id="about" class="py-12 sm:py-16 scroll-mt-16">
  <div class="space-y-6">
    <div class="flex items-end justify-between gap-4">
      <div class="space-y-1">
        <h2
          class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-(--color-text-primary)"
          style="font-family: var(--font-heading);"
        >
          About Me
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-(--color-text-secondary)">A bit about my background and what drives my engineering work.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="hidden sm:block pb-1" title="Badtz-Maru mascot">
        <BadtzMaru size={56} />
      </div>
    </div>

    <XPWindow title="about_ethan.txt - Notepad" icon="📝" menuBar={true}>
      <div class="space-y-4 text-(--color-text-primary) text-base max-w-none leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          I'm a final-year Computer Science student at the University of Nottingham, on track for a First, returning from a 13-month software engineering placement at Siemens Digital Industries Software. Before the placement, most of my code lived in university coursework with mock data, dummy users, and "close enough" logic. Stepping into a massive production codebase was a wake-up call in the best way. Over 100 pull requests later, I went from fixing one-liners and figuring out where things lived to shipping cross-system features, reviewing colleagues' pull requests, and helping interview and train the next intern cohort. I learned quickly that writing the code is often the straightforward part; the real engineering is understanding how the wider system fits together and building software that holds up in the hands of real users.
        </p>
        <p>
          Outside of the placement, I like exploring different corners of computing. That has ranged from low-level systems programming in C (writing a process scheduler and a concurrent TCP routing server) to machine learning and hackathons. At LeedsHack 2026, my team and I built Mindmaply in 24 hours, a branching conversational tool using graph data structures and the Gemini API, which won "Best Use of Google Gemini API". I enjoy diving into unfamiliar tech, figuring out how things work under the hood, and seeing an idea go from a rough concept to a working prototype.
        </p>
        <p>
          When I'm not at my desk, I spend a lot of time in the gym training for competitive powerlifting. Methodically logging sessions, refining form, and chipping away at numbers week over week has taught me patience, and those habits carry right over into how I debug and write software. I also play poker and chess (enjoying games where probability and decision-making matter), and listen to an eclectic mix of game soundtracks, jazz, and heavy rock. Now that I'm back in Nottingham for my final year, I'm focused on finishing strong and looking forward to 2027 graduate roles where I can keep tackling interesting problems with a great team.
        </p>
      </div>
    </XPWindow>
  </div>
</section>
